course: dictionary
lesson: 3108
title: Meet the eland
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can be the name of a real animal. When you meet a new animal name, read the words around it, because they often give clues. «The Giraffe, the Zebra, the Eland, the Koodoo (kudu) and the Hartebeest lived there» shows that eland is the name of an animal, since it sits in a list of animals.
explain: The brackets in that sentence are a clue too. «Koodoo (kudu)» tells you the two spellings name the same animal, so you do not need to worry that the word looks different.
explain: Story animals and real animals can share names. In «How the Leopard Got His Spots», the Eland, the Kudu and the Zebra are hunted, and «the Eland and the Koodoo grew darker, with little wavy grey lines on their backs». That is a made-up Just So Story, so use a dictionary or a science book for true facts about elands.
fact: An eland is a very large African antelope.
fact: Male and female elands both have spiral horns.
fact: Elands are light brown and have a hanging fold of skin on the throat.
fact: The eland is the largest antelope in the world.
fact: «Eland», «kudu» and «hartebeest» are real African antelopes, and «Koodoo» is another spelling of kudu.
exercise: What is an eland? | A cold river fish | *A very large African antelope | A kind of tree that grows on the veld | A small bird with a long tail
exercise: What do male and female elands both have? | A long striped tail | Wings to fly | Only the males have horns | *Spiral horns
exercise: Which animal in this list is NOT an antelope? «The Eland, the Kudu, the Hartebeest and the Zebra.» | Kudu | *Zebra | Eland | Hartebeest
test: What hangs from an eland's throat? | Its horns | Its tail | *A fold of skin
test: What is special about the eland? | It can fly like a bird | *It is the largest antelope in the world | It is the smallest antelope in the world
exam: Is the leopard hunt in «How the Leopard Got His Spots» real science? | *No, it is a made-up Just So Story | No, because leopards cannot hunt | Yes, it is a true science report | Yes, Kipling saw it happen
